    "content": "## Establishing Airtight Purchase Specifications for Dehydrated Allium\n\nCommercial disputes in the international spice and dehydrated vegetable trade almost always trace back to imprecise contractual specifications. Simply ordering \"standard red onion powder\" allows unscrupulous vendors to supply batches with elevated moisture, excessive acid-insoluble ash (sand), burnt specks, or high microbial bio-burden.\n\nAt **AVANI AGRO FOODS**, our trade desk coordinates commercial consignments against verified Technical Data Sheets (TDS) backed by independent accredited testing.\n\nThis guide provides quality assurance directors and procurement managers with the exact physical, chemical, and microbiological parameters to include in your legal purchase agreements.\n\n---\n\n## Master Technical Specification Matrix: Dehydrated Red Onion Powder\n\n```\n===================================================================================\nPRODUCT IDENTITY\nBotanical Name:      Allium cepa L.\nPart Utilized:       100% Dehydrated Fleshy Scale Leaves (Roots & Papery Skin Excluded)\nCountry of Origin:   India (Sourced from Maharashtra Agricultural Belt)\nHS Tariff Code:      0712.20.00\nProcessing Method:   Pneumatic Peeling -> Hydro-Wash -> Continuous Belt Dehydration ->\n                     Optical Color Sorting -> Stainless Milling -> 10,000 Gauss Traps\n===================================================================================\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Physical & Organoleptic Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Standard Export Specification | Analytical Test Protocol |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Appearance** | Fine, free-flowing, homogeneous powder | Visual Examination |\n| **Color** | Pinkish-buff to light reddish-tan | Hunter Lab Colorimeter |\n| **Aroma** | Pungent, sharp, characteristic sweet-allium | Sensory Organoleptic Panel |\n| **Taste** | Sharp, savory, characteristic onion bite | Sensory Organoleptic Panel |\n| **Particle Mesh Size** | **80–100 Mesh** (≥ 95% passing 150µm) | ASTM E11 Sieve Shaker Analysis |\n| **Alternative Mesh** | **60–80 Mesh** (Granular, ≥ 95% pass) | ASTM E11 Sieve Shaker Analysis |\n| **Foreign Matter** | Nil / Non-Detectable | Visual / Gravimetric Flotation |\n| **Extraneous Metal**| Nil (Inline 10,000 Gauss magnetic trap) | Metal Detection (Fe 1.5mm, SS 2.0mm)|\n\n---\n\n## 2. Chemical & Purity Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Regulatory Target | Contractual Maximum | Analytical Method |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Moisture Content** | 4.5% – 5.5% | **≤ 6.0% Max** | Halogen / Karl Fischer (AOAC 930.15) |\n| **Total Ash** | 3.5% – 4.5% | **≤ 5.0% Max** | Muffle Furnace (AOAC 942.05) |\n| **Acid-Insoluble Ash** | 0.1% – 0.3% | **≤ 0.5% Max** | ISO 1577 (Sand / Silica Index) |\n| **Sulfur Dioxide (SO2)**| Non-Detectable (< 10 ppm)| **< 50 ppm** (or specified) | Monier-Williams Method |\n| **Pyruvic Acid Content**| 10.0 – 14.0 µmol/g | N/A (Minimum target) | DNPH Colorimetric Assay |\n\n*Procurement Tip:* Acid-insoluble ash is the definitive indicator of root dirt and sand. Cheap commodity powder often exhibits acid-insoluble ash above 1.5%, which causes consumer complaints of gritty mouthfeel in sauces.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Microbiological Pathogen Criteria\n\nDehydrated vegetables require stringent microbiological testing:\n\n| Microbiological Assay | Food-Grade Standard | Stringent Commercial Standard | Method Reference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Total Plate Count (TPC)** | ≤ 100,000 CFU/g | ≤ 50,000 CFU/g | ISO 4833-1 / FDA BAM Ch. 3 |\n| **Yeast and Mould** | ≤ 1,000 CFU/g | ≤ 500 CFU/g | ISO 21527-2 / FDA BAM Ch. 18 |\n| **Coliforms** | ≤ 100 CFU/g | ≤ 50 CFU/g | ISO 4832 / FDA BAM Ch. 4 |\n| **Escherichia coli** | Negative in 10g | Absent in 10g | ISO 16649-2 / FDA BAM Ch. 4 |\n| **Salmonella spp.** | **Negative in 25g** | **Negative in 25g (Zero Tol.)**| ISO 6579-1 / FDA BAM Ch. 5 |\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | Negative in 10g | Negative in 10g | ISO 6888-1 / FDA BAM Ch. 12 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Heavy Metals Contaminant Thresholds (ICP-MS)\n\n- **Lead (Pb):** ≤ 2.0 ppm (mg/kg).\n- **Cadmium (Cd):** ≤ 1.0 ppm (mg/kg).\n- **Arsenic (As):** ≤ 1.0 ppm (mg/kg).\n- **Mercury (Hg):** ≤ 0.1 ppm (mg/kg).\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)\n\n### What causes red onion powder to darken over time?\nNon-enzymatic browning (the Maillard reaction between natural reducing sugars and amino acids) occurs if storage temperatures exceed 25°C or if initial product moisture was above 6.0%. Vacuum sealing or nitrogen flushing inside moisture-barrier liners halts this reaction.\n\n### How is sulfur dioxide (SO2) controlled in processing?\nOur partner facilities specialize in non-sulfited dehydration where fresh onions are dried without sulfur dioxide gas treatment, yielding natural powder compliant with EU and US clean-label standards.\n\n---\n\n## Download Our Official Specification Dossier\n\nDemand verifiable standards for your agricultural supply chain.\n- Review technical data in our [Red Onion Powder Catalog](/catalog/red-onion-powder).\n- Compare with our [Moringa Powder Technical Catalog](/catalog/moringa-powder).\n- [Request a Certificate of Analysis Dossier](/contact?product=onion&type=quote) from Sachin Shinde at AVANI AGRO FOODS.\n"
